   110	
   111	## 1. Canonical payload bytes: RFC 8785 over the accepted domain
   112	
   113	### 1.1 The accepted value domain V
   114	
   115	A JSON value is in V if and only if all of the following hold.
   116	- **Literals.** `null`, `true` and `false`.
   117	- **Numbers.** A finite IEEE 754 binary64 value other than −0. *Reason: this is exactly what RFC 8785 can
   118	  serialize without loss.*
   119	- **Strings, including member names.** A sequence of Unicode scalar values with:
   120	  - no surrogate code point (U+D800 to U+DFFF), and so no lone `\uD8xx` escape;
   121	  - no noncharacter (U+FDD0 to U+FDEF, and U+xFFFE or U+xFFFF in every plane). *Reason: RFC 7493 §2.1 (I-JSON); G-04.*
   122	  - no code point that is unassigned (General_Category `Cn`) in **Unicode 16.0.0**. *Reason: this pins the NFC test
   123	    (next item) to one table. Under Unicode's stability policy, a string built from characters assigned in 16.0.0
   124	    that is NFC under 16.0.0 stays NFC under every later version. An unassigned code point carries no such
   125	    guarantee, so two verifiers on different Unicode versions could disagree about it.*
   126	  - the string already in NFC. It is **rejected, never normalized**. *Reason: R-3.4, "inputs relying on Unicode
   127	    normalization assumptions"; G-05 kept.*
   128	- **Arrays.** Arrays of members of V.
   129	- **Objects.** Objects with pairwise distinct member names, compared after unescaping. *Reason: R-3.4's duplicate
   130	  names; G-04.*
   131	- **Depth.** Nesting depth is at most **64** for a whole payload. Each array or object adds 1. The requester's
   132	  `content` sits at depth 3, so it may nest at most **62** levels. *Reason: this pins a limit that real parsers
   133	  otherwise set differently.*
   134	- **Size.** A payload is at most **2^24 bytes** (16 MiB). *Reason: a fixed cap that both implementations
   135	  enforce. No V1 payload comes near it.*
   136	
   137	### 1.2 The number rule: value-preserving (G-03 and G-56 resolved for V1)
   138	
   139	A number **literal** `L` (RFC 8259 grammar) in submitted JSON text is accepted if and only if:
   140	1. L is not a negative zero: no `-` with zero value (`-0`, `-0.0`, `-0e5`). *Reason: the R-3.4 list.*
   141	2. `d` = the binary64 nearest to L's exact decimal value, under round-half-even, is finite. Overflow is rejected.
   142	3. **The exact decimal value of `ES(d)` equals the exact decimal value of L.** `ES` is ECMAScript
   143	   Number::toString, the serialization RFC 8785 §3.2.2.3 uses.
   144	
   145	Accepted: `9.8`, `9.80`, `1E2`, `1e21`, `5e-324`, `123456789012345680000` and `0e99999999999999999999`. Each keeps its
   146	value, and only the spelling changes.
   147	
   148	Rejected, each with its reason:
   149	- `1e400`: overflow.
   150	- `1e-400`: nonzero, and rounds to 0.
   151	- `333333333.33333329` and `9007199254740993`: the value would change.
   152	- `0.1000000000000000055511151231257827021181583404541015625`: this is *exactly* a double, but RFC 8785 prints it as
   153	  `0.1`, which has a different value.
   154	- `4.9e-324`: rounds to the smallest subnormal, which prints as `5e-324`.
   155	- `-0`.
   156	- `NaN` and `Infinity`: not JSON at all.
   157	
   158	Subnormals that survive rule 3 are accepted, so G-03b is reversed: RFC 8785 treats them as ordinary.
   159	
   160	**Why this rule and not the registered literal rule.** Read literally, "exactly representable as a double" does
   161	not compose with RFC 8785. It *accepts* `0.1000000000000000055511151231257827021181583404541015625`, whose
   162	canonical form `0.1` it then *rejects* when the verifier re-reads the payload. So under that reading,
   163	`canonical()` maps its own domain outside itself. The value-preserving rule is the largest rule under which:
   164	- (a) canonicalization never changes a value; and
   165	- (b) every canonical output is itself accepted.
   166	**This changes A1 P8's rejection item and prereg §4.3's wording. A9.8 registers it for `tessera-v1-strict`,
   167	replacing both (A9.7 rows 22–23).** [OWNER 2026-10-19, G38] *(Was: "… and it needs an author sentence (§9, D-1). The
   168	V1 derivation's profile line, 'registered number rule unchanged', must be reconciled with it either way." A9.8
   169	supplied the sentence, and the derivation's line now states the value-preserving rule.)*
   170	
   171	What "never helpfully normalized" means here. The **issuer's ingestion** normalizes *spelling only*:
   172	- insignificant whitespace;
   173	- member order;
   174	- escape choice;
   175	- number spelling.
   176	
   177	It never changes a value. The **verifier normalizes nothing**: a payload must already equal `JCS` of its own
   178	parse (§1.4, `JSON_NONCANONICAL`). Injectivity (PROOF.md) is stated over *values*. Two texts that differ only in
   179	spelling are one value by definition, and they get one canonical byte string. That is intended.
   180	
   181	### 1.3 Serialization
   182	
   183	`canonical(v) = JCS(v)` (RFC 8785):
   184	- UTF-8 output, with no insignificant whitespace;
   185	- object members sorted by the UTF-16 code units of their names;
   186	- numbers printed by `ES`;
   187	- strings escaped only for `"`, `\` and U+0000 to U+001F: `\b \t \n \f \r` where defined, `\u00xx` with
   188	  lowercase hex otherwise. Every other scalar appears literally.
   189	
   190	*Reason: R-3.1 binds to RFC 8785. Vectors C-04 and C-05 pin the escape and the UTF-16 ordering traps, for example
   191	U+1F600 sorting before U+E000.*
   192	
   193	### 1.4 Decoding, and the order in which rejections are reported
   194	
   195	Two entry points.
   196	- `ingest(text)` takes requester JSON: whitespace is allowed, and the depth limit is 62.
   197	- `payload(bytes)` takes in-frame bytes: the depth limit is 64, and the bytes must be canonical.
   198	
   199	Each runs these checks in this order and reports the **first category that fails**, scanning the whole value for
   200	each category. *Reason: both implementations then agree on the reason code, not only on rejection.*
   201	
   202	1. `JSON_ENCODING`: the bytes are not strict UTF-8. Overlongs, encoded surrogates and values above U+10FFFF are
   203	   rejected.
   204	2. `JSON_SYNTAX`:
   205	   - a BOM; not exactly one RFC 8259 JSON text; trailing non-whitespace;
   206	   - a raw control character in a string; bad escapes; leading zeros; `+`; `.5`; `1.`; `NaN`; `Infinity`.
   207	3. `JSON_DEPTH`.
   208	4. `JSON_DUPLICATE_NAME`.
   209	5. `JSON_STRING`: a surrogate, a noncharacter, an unassigned code point, or text that is not NFC.
   210	6. `JSON_NUMBER`: §1.2.
   211	7. *(`payload` only)* `JSON_NONCANONICAL`: the bytes are not equal to `JCS` of the parsed value.
   212	
   213	---
   214	
   215	## 2. The tagged byte strings: one header, four tags
   216	
   217	Every byte string that a V1 slot key signs (attestation and manifest frames), and every receipt-anchor and snapshot
   218	preimage that OTS stamps, begins with the same header. Two V1 byte strings carry no header: the git channel's
   219	stamped message, which is exactly the 40 lowercase hex digits of the signed commit id followed by one LF (A9.5,
   220	§7.2), and the key-publication value (§7), which the repository key and the DNSSEC keys sign through git and DNS.
   221	Neither is a frame (Remark 10 of PROOF.md). [OWNER 2026-10-19, Levadura C6] *(Was: "Every V1 byte string that a V1
   222	key signs or that OTS stamps begins with the same header:", which the git stamped message contradicts.)*
   223	
   224	The header:
   225	
   226	```
   227	header(t) = u8 len(t) || t                       t ∈ T (ASCII, no NUL)
   228	```
   229	
   230	| Tag `t` | len | First bytes (hex) | Construction |
   231	|---|---|---|---|
   232	| `tessera-v1/attest` | 17 | `11 74 65 73 …` | frame, §3 |
   233	| `tessera-v1/manifest` | 19 | `13 74 65 73 …` | frame, §4 |
   234	| `tessera-v1/receipt-anchor-sig` | 29 | `1d 74 65 73 …` | signature-inclusive anchor preimage `A_sig`, §5 |
   235	| `tessera-v1/dnssec-snapshot` | 26 | `1a 74 65 73 …` | snapshot preimage, §6 |
   236	
   237	`tessera-v1/receipt-anchor` (25, `19 …`), the frame-only anchor tag of the 2026-10-09 draft, is **withdrawn**. It
   238	is not in T, so it is rejected with `TAG_UNKNOWN` (§5.1).
   239	
   240	- One string serves as both the domain-separation tag and the object type. R-3.11 lists the two as separate items,
   241	  but A1 P8's four-field frame has a single slot. The prefix `tessera-v1/` does the domain separation, and the
   242	  suffix names the type (G-07 resolved).
   243	- A tag outside T is rejected with `TAG_UNKNOWN`. That includes P7 types V1 does not use (`tessera-v1/wrapper`)
   244	  and later versions (`tessera-v2/attest`). A tag in T that is not the expected one is rejected with
   245	  `TAG_MISMATCH`. *Reason: these are the registered "type-tag mismatch" rejections, and the tag decides the
   246	  construction before any other byte is read.*
   247	- The length byte makes T prefix-free as headers. The proof's Lemma 4 depends on that.
   248	- *Why ASCII tags:* a reader with no V1 software can see them in a hex dump. That helps C6.
   249	
   250	---
   251	
   252	## 3. The attestation frame (one per signer)
   253	
   254	### 3.1 Layout (the four registered fields, A1 P8 / A7 §A7.1)
   255	
   256	```
   257	frame(t, p) = header(t) || u16 canonicalization_version || u32 payload_length || payload
   258	            with canonicalization_version = 0x0001 and payload_length = len(payload) ≤ 2^24
   259	```
   260	
   261	| Offset | Width | Field | Value / rule | Reason |
   262	|---|---|---|---|---|
   263	| 0 | 1 | tag length | `0x11` for attest | prefix-free tags |
   264	| 1 | 17 | `type_tag` | `tessera-v1/attest` | §2 |
   265	| 18 | 2 | `canonicalization_version` | `0x0001` means RFC 8785 + §1's domain + §1.2's number rule. Any other value: `CV_UNKNOWN` | fixed width; G-02 resolved (u16, value 1) |
   266	| 20 | 4 | `payload_length` | the exact payload length, ≤ 2^24, else `LENGTH_CAP` | G-01 changed from u64 to u32. Four bytes cover the cap, and a smaller field is one less thing to get wrong |
   267	| 24 | L | `payload` | `JCS(value)`, §3.2 | A7 §A7.1: the canonical payload bytes |
   268	
   269	The frame ends **exactly** at offset 24 + L.
   270	- If fewer than L payload bytes remain, the result is `TRUNCATED`. Truncation inside the header also gives
   271	  `TRUNCATED`.
   272	- If more remain, the result is `TRAILING`.
   273	
   274	The registered rejections "length-prefix mismatch" and "trailing bytes" are therefore one condition, reported by
   275	its direction. Once the length is the only payload boundary, a short length and a trailing byte are the same
   276	event.
   277	
   278	### 3.2 The attestation payload
   279	
   280	The payload is exactly this object. **No member may be missing or added** (`SCHEMA`). *Reason: a closed schema
   281	means no unsigned or unknown field can carry meaning, which is R-3.39.*
   282	
   283	```
   284	{"common":{
   285	   "alg":                 A,     ^[A-Z0-9][A-Z0-9-]{0,63}$          V1 issues the catalog id (see below)
   286	   "binding_forms":       [F…],  nonempty, strictly ascending, each ^[A-Z][A-Z0-9_]{0,31}$
   287	   "content":             <any value in V>                          the requester's content
   288	   "declared_issue_time": "YYYY-MM-DDThh:mm:ssZ"                    a real UTC date and time, seconds 00–59
   289	   "issuer":              D,     lowercase LDH name, ≥ 2 labels, labels 1–63, total ≤ 241
   290	   "manifest_sha256":     hex64  hex(SHA256(manifest frame)), §4
   291	   "preamble_sha256":     hex64  hex(SHA256(purpose-preamble bytes))
   292	   "profile":             "tessera-v1-strict"                       exact
   293	   "spec":{"id": S,  ^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9._/-]{0,127}$                  V1: "tessera-v1-strict/verification-spec/1"
   294	           "sha256": hex64}      hex(SHA256(specification bytes))
   295	   "vectors_sha256":      hex64  hex(SHA256(conformance-vector bytes))
   296	 },
   297	 "signer":{"key_fp": FP}}        FP ∈ §3.3
   298	```
   299	
   300	`hex64` means exactly 64 **lowercase** hex digits. Members appear in JCS order, which is the order shown.
   301	
   302	- **The id V1 issues.** Under the registered hybrid branch (A9.6; HYBRID-SUPPLEMENT §1, registered 2026-10-16), V1
   303	  issues the pair id `KMS-MLDSA65-PURE-GPG-OPENPGP4-ECDSA-P256-SHA256` (§5.2). The classical id
   304	  `OPENPGP4-ECDSA-P256-SHA256` stays codec-known (rule O in both slots) and is policy-unsupported in the V1
   305	  installation: `UNVERIFIABLE / ALG_UNKNOWN` (V1-PROFILE §1, §8). [OWNER 2026-10-19, Levadura C2] *(Was: `V1 issues
   306	  "OPENPGP4-ECDSA-P256-SHA256"`, the fallback branch's id; the supplement's superseded-statement list omitted it.)*
   307	- **Field grammars** [OWNER 2026-10-19, G18; codifies what both codecs already do, no vector change].
   308	  `declared_issue_time`: years 0001–9999, proleptic Gregorian, seconds 00–59. `issuer`: labels match
   309	  `[a-z0-9-]{1,63}` with no leading or trailing hyphen; all-numeric labels, the top label included, are allowed; at
   310	  least two labels; at most 241 bytes in total.
   311	
   312	| Field | Reason, and the G-id it resolves |
   313	|---|---|
   314	| `alg`: any well-formed id, not only V1's | P8 accepts a well-formed unknown id, and stage (c) returns `UNVERIFIABLE`. The canary (R-3.53) needs this, and G-10b's failure mode is avoided. The id names the scheme: "a v4 OpenPGP binary-document signature whose document is the frame, ECDSA P-256, SHA-256" (G-57 kept). Its location inside the payload is A3 §A3.6.1's. G-11 is resolved for V1. |
   315	| `binding_forms`: a sorted, unique, nonempty array | R-3.37 says P8 "defines the set's canonical encoding, ordering, uniqueness and bounds". It sits in the attestation's signed bytes (G-17 kept). *Which* forms V1 requires is C4 profile work. The vectors use `["STMT_DIRECT"]` for illustration. |
   316	| `declared_issue_time`: second precision, `Z` | One spelling per instant. No leap second, because Unix time and Bitcoin time have none. G-16 kept. |
   317	| `issuer` = D, at most 241 characters | D is bound in the signed bytes for the owner-name check (C4, G-21). 241 keeps `_tessera-v1.<D>` within DNS's 255-octet name limit (§7). |
   318	| `manifest_sha256` over the manifest **frame** | The hash covers a tagged string, so it is domain-separated by construction. G-14 is resolved: the hash covers the frame of the tuple and nothing more. |
   319	| `spec`, `vectors_sha256`, `preamble_sha256` | R-3.50: the spec id and hash are in the signed bytes. The vectors and preamble are bound the same way. Each hash is over the object's raw bytes, so `sha256sum` reproduces it with no Tessera code. G-18 and G-47 are resolved as far as P8 goes: they are bound. Consuming them is verifier work. |
   320	| `profile` inside `common` | The signed bytes name their own profile (C6). Dispatch reads the bundle's unsigned profile label first (§8). |
   321	| `content` is arbitrary V | Any JSON value in V. A bare digest string is accepted (C-06 and F-03). |
   322	
   323	### 3.3 Fingerprint slot encodings (G-10 resolved; owner ruling for the DNS record)
   324	
   325	| Slot | `key_fp` string | Definition |
   326	|---|---|---|
   327	| `kms` | `spki-sha256:` + 64 hex | hex(SHA256(DER SubjectPublicKeyInfo)). For P-256 the SPKI is the 26-byte prefix `3059301306072a8648ce3d020106082a8648ce3d030107034200` followed by `04‖X‖Y` (91 bytes). This is what KMS `GetPublicKey` returns. |
   328	| `gpg` | `gpg-sha256:` + 64 hex | hex(SHA256(body)), where `body` is the v4 public-key packet body (RFC 9580 §5.5.2): `04` ‖ u32 creation ‖ `13` ‖ `08 2a8648ce3d030107` ‖ u16 `0x0203` ‖ `04‖X‖Y`. These are exactly the bytes that the v4 fingerprint hashes *after* its `0x99` ‖ u16 length prefix. That prefix is **not** included, and SHA-1 is not used. This is **not** the fingerprint gpg prints; a tool computes it from the exported key packet. |
   329	
   330	- The scheme prefix makes the two slots' strings differ in their first byte, so they can never be equal. It also
   331	  makes the frame self-describing.
   332	- The DNS record (§7) carries the bare hex under the slot name. The verifier compares `kms=<h>` with
   333	  `spki-sha256:<h>` and `gpg=<h>` with `gpg-sha256:<h>`.
   334	- *Consequences to record:*
   335	  - V1 uses no SHA-1 for key binding. The gpg slot binds the whole key packet body, creation time included, under
   336	    SHA-256.
   337	  - The KMS key's OpenPGP key-packet creation time is **not** covered by its `spki-sha256` fingerprint. This is
   338	    **accepted**: for the KMS slot the verifier binds the SPKI (the key KMS actually holds), not the OpenPGP key
   339	    packet. The OpenPGP wrapper's creation time therefore carries no meaning for that slot.
   340	
   341	### 3.4 The two frames, as bytes (A5 §A5.4 and R-3.12 stated over bytes)
   342	
   343	For one receipt with common value `C`:
   344	
   345	```
   346	F_kms = 11 'tessera-v1/attest' 0001 u32(L_kms) '{"common":' JCS(C) ',"signer":{"key_fp":' JCS(fp_kms) '}}'
   347	F_gpg = 11 'tessera-v1/attest' 0001 u32(L_gpg) '{"common":' JCS(C) ',"signer":{"key_fp":' JCS(fp_gpg) '}}'
   348	```
   349	
   350	- `JCS(C)` occupies bytes **[34, 34 + |JCS(C)|)** of both frames and is byte-identical. That covers the requester
   351	  content and every common binding.
   352	- The frames differ only in bytes 20–23 (the length) and in the `signer` member. In F-01/F-02 they first differ
   353	  at offset 23, then from offset 745, and the lengths differ by 1 byte (the prefixes `spki-sha256:` and
   354	  `gpg-sha256:`).
   355	- Payload-byte identity of `common` is therefore **not** frame equality. The two frames are never equal (C1).
   356	- No slot name is written: A5 §A5.4 says only the fingerprint differs. The slot comes from the manifest's order:
   357	  `key_fp` is slot `kms` and `sset[0]` is slot `gpg` (G-09 kept). A frame in the wrong slot gives
   358	  `RECEIPT_SLOT`.
   359	
   360	### 3.5 What exactly is signed (OpenPGP trailer, PHASE3 finding 4)
   361	
   362	Under `alg = OPENPGP4-ECDSA-P256-SHA256`, each slot's signature is an OpenPGP v4 signature of **type 0x00
   363	(binary document)** whose document is the frame. Its exact byte serialization is fixed by §5.2, rule O. A raw
   364	FIPS 204/205 signer signs the frame itself (§5.2, rule R). For OpenPGP, the signed hash input is
   365	`frame ‖ trailer`, where:
   366	
   367	```
   368	trailer = 04 ‖ 00 ‖ pubalg ‖ hashalg ‖ u16 hlen ‖ hashed-subpackets ‖ 04 FF ‖ u32(6 + hlen)
   369	```
   370	
   371	- The trailer's last four bytes give its own length, so `frame ‖ trailer` splits into frame and trailer reading
   372	  from the end, and the frame's own `payload_length` then delimits the payload. PROOF.md Lemma 7 covers this.
   373	- **Signature type 0x01 (text) must be rejected:** text mode rewrites line endings, and frame headers and lengths
   374	  can contain the bytes `0x0a` and `0x0d`.
   375	- The unhashed subpacket area carries no meaning. Its exact bytes are nonetheless committed by `A_sig` (§5), as
   376	  are all of the signature's bytes.
   377	- **Identifier rule (W1, revised 2026-10-12, finding 5).** No V1 semantic, standing or attempt identifier is
   378	  derived from signature bytes. The anchor digest `SHA256(A_sig)` does commit to them, by design. It is an
   379	  existence commitment for this receipt's exact bytes, not an identifier of the statement (§8).
   380	- **Malleability now means exact-copy integrity, not forgery.** A signature can be changed and still verify:
   381	  - ECDSA's `(r, n − s)`;
   382	  - an edited unhashed subpacket;
   383	  - another packet serialization;
   384	  - a fresh randomized signature over the same frame.
   385	
   386	  Any such change gives bytes that fail the stamped anchor (`RECEIPT_ANCHOR_MISMATCH`, negative controls XR-10
   387	  to XR-14). So none of them lets a new signature inherit an old time bound.
   388	  - What remains is copy integrity. A mirror that alters a signature byte damages its copy, just as altering a
   389	    frame byte would. Preservation must keep the committed bytes exactly.
   390	  - A later re-stamp of an altered copy gets a new, later time bound. It does not restore the original one.
   391	  - V1 does not require signatures to be unique, and it normalizes no signature (low-s or otherwise). Low-s would
   392	    not cover OpenPGP serialization or unhashed-area changes, and any normalization after stamping would break the
   393	    anchor. Serialization choices are made before stamping (§5.4).
   394	
   395	---
   396	
   397	## 4. The manifest frame (map-v1 tuple; G-08, G-14, G-22)
   398	
   399	```
   400	F_manifest = frame("tessera-v1/manifest", JCS({"alg":A,"issuer":D,"key_fp":fp_kms,"sset":[fp_gpg],"ver":"map-v1"}))
   401	manifest_sha256 = hex(SHA256(F_manifest))
   402	```
   403	
   404	- The schema is closed: `key_fp` must be a `spki-sha256:` string, and `sset` must hold exactly one `gpg-sha256:`
   405	  string. `ver` is exactly `map-v1`. Anything else is `SCHEMA`.
   406	- *Reason: this is the map-v1 tuple of R-3.36 in the slot order of the C-C2 trace (G-09). Each key's possession
   407	  proof (R-3.38) is its OpenPGP signature over this same frame. The `tessera-v1/manifest` tag keeps that
   408	  signature apart from the same key's attestation signature (P7).*
   409	
   410	## 5. The receipt-anchor preimage `A_sig` (G-19 and G-59 resolved; K-2; revised 2026-10-12)
   411	
   412	```
   413	A_sig = header("tessera-v1/receipt-anchor-sig") ‖ u8 0x02
   414	        ‖ u32 len(F_kms) ‖ F_kms ‖ u32 len(S_kms) ‖ S_kms
   415	        ‖ u32 len(F_gpg) ‖ F_gpg ‖ u32 len(S_gpg) ‖ S_gpg
   416	anchor digest = SHA256(A_sig), the value OpenTimestamps stamps
   417	```
   418	
   419	- `S_x` is slot x's detached signature over `F_x`, exactly as §5.2 serializes it.
   420	- Slots appear in **manifest slot order**: `kms` is the manifest's `key_fp`, and `gpg` is `sset[0]`.
   421	- The count byte is the number of slots. It must be 2 (`ANCHOR_COUNT`).
   422	
   423	**Decoding, slot by slot.** Each step's boundary is fixed by bytes already read.
   424	1. `u32` frame length. Above 2^24 + 64 gives `LENGTH_CAP`. Then exactly that many bytes, decoded as one complete
   425	   attestation frame (§3.1). That decode yields the frame's `common.alg`.
   426	2. `u32` signature length. 0 gives `SIG_EMPTY`. Above the cap of the rule for `(common.alg, slot)` gives
   427	   `LENGTH_CAP`.
   428	3. Exactly that many bytes, decoded by that rule (§5.2).
   429	4. After the second signature, any byte gives `TRAILING`. A slot running past the end of input gives
   430	   `TRUNCATED`.
   431	
   432	The rule for a slot is chosen from that slot's own frame and its position, both already read.
   433	
   434	*Reason.* One OTS proof must cover a receipt that has two signed frames (R-3.42 with reading (a)). It must commit
   435	to the signatures, not only the frames: a stamp over unsigned frames proves only that the frames existed. It
   436	leaves open signing them later, once signing capability for every required slot has been obtained (CODEX-REVIEW
   437	finding 1; finding 2 for "every slot"). Length prefixes and whole, exactly consumed parts make the preimage
   438	injective without any hash assumption (PROOF Lemma 7). The tag keeps it from being taken for a snapshot's proof.
   439	
   440	**Outside the preimage.**
   441	- The outputs of anchoring: the OTS proof, txid, block headers and confirmation evidence. Including them would
   442	  be circular (finding 4).
   443	- Transport decoration: armor, containers and the bundle format.
   444	
   445	### 5.1 Why the tag changes (and the count byte does not)
   446	
   447	The 2026-10-09 frame-only layout used the tag `tessera-v1/receipt-anchor`. That tag is **withdrawn and removed
   448	from T**. A frame-only preimage is therefore rejected at its header with `TAG_UNKNOWN` (XA-09). A fully signed
   449	receipt presented with a frame-only anchor is rejected the same way (XR-09).
   450	
   451	There is **no fallback**. No verifier accepts a frame-only anchor under any profile label.
   452	
   453	Reasons:
   454	- **Separation by construction.** With a distinct tag, rejecting the old layout is PROOF Lemma 4: the header
   455	  alone decides it. It does not depend on whether bytes in the old layout happen to fail a signature rule.
   456	  - Under the old tag, that dependence would be real. Under rule R, any byte string of the right length is a
   457	    syntactically valid signature.
   458	  - XA-10 shows the same-tag case: the old layout is rejected today only because the gpg frame's first byte
   459	    fails rule O's packet header.
   460	- **Readable dumps.** A hex dump names which construction it holds (C6).
   461	- **No migration cost.** Nothing was issued under the draft layout, so replacing it costs nothing.
   462	
   463	The count byte keeps its meaning (two slots), so it is not the place to carry the version.
   464	
   465	### 5.2 Signature-slot rules (by algorithm identifier)
   466	
   467	Each slot's `S` is **exactly one detached signature** in the single serialization its rule pins.
   468	- `S` is **nonempty** and **at most the rule's cap**.
   469	- The decoder **consumes `S` exactly**. Bytes beyond the signature's own extent give `TRAILING`, which covers a
   470	  second packet. Fewer bytes give `TRUNCATED`.
   471	- **Never `S`:** ASCII armor, a display string, a keyring, or any container that can hold more than one
   472	  signature.
   473	
   474	The rule is chosen by `(common.alg, slot)`:
   475	
   476	| `common.alg` | slot `kms` | slot `gpg` | Cap per `S` |
   477	|---|---|---|---|
   478	| `OPENPGP4-ECDSA-P256-SHA256` (the classical V1 signers) | rule O | rule O | 1024 bytes |
   479	| `KMS-MLDSA65-PURE-GPG-OPENPGP4-ECDSA-P256-SHA256` (hybrid pair id; registered by the branch-registration supplement of 2026-10-16, A9.6) | rule R: ML-DSA-65 (FIPS 204), pure, empty context | rule O (OpenPGP P-256; slot-2 custody per A9.6) | `kms`: exactly 3309; `gpg`: 1024 |
   480	| any other well-formed id (§3.2) | opaque | opaque | 1 to 65536 bytes; no inner rule. Stage (c) gives `UNVERIFIABLE` (canary, A-02) |
   481	
   482	**Rule O (OpenPGP v4 signature, ECDSA P-256, SHA-256; RFC 9580 §4.2, §5.2.3).**
   483	
   484	*The packet header (`SIG_PACKET`).*
   485	- `S` is exactly one Signature packet (tag 2) with a **legacy-format header in its shortest form**:
   486	  - `0x88 ‖ u8 n` when the body has n ≤ 255 bytes;
   487	  - `0x89 ‖ u16 n` when 256 ≤ n ≤ 1021.
   488	- Each of these gives `SIG_PACKET`:
   489	  - any other first byte: an OpenPGP-format `0xC2` header, another packet tag, or ASCII armor's `-`;
   490	  - a longer length form than needed.
   491	- This also excludes partial and indeterminate lengths.
   492	
   493	*The packet body (`SIG_FORMAT`).* The body is, in order:
   494	1. version `04`, type `00`, public-key algorithm `13` (19, ECDSA), hash algorithm `08` (SHA-256);
   495	2. `u16` hashed length, then the hashed subpackets;
   496	3. `u16` unhashed length, then the unhashed subpackets;
   497	4. two bytes: the left 16 bits of the digest;
   498	5. MPI r;
   499	6. MPI s.
   500	
   501	Each of the following holds, or the result is `SIG_FORMAT`:
   502	- each subpacket area is an exact sequence of subpackets, each at least 1 byte, with shortest-form lengths. The
   503	  length forms are RFC 9580 §5.2.3.7's: first octet < 192, one octet; 192–254, two octets
   504	  `((o1−192)<<8)+o2+192` (192–16319); 255, then a four-octet big-endian value (five octets in all). The length
   505	  counts the type octet and is ≥ 1. A longer form than needed is `SIG_FORMAT`; this is a V1 rule, not an RFC ban.
   506	  [OWNER 2026-10-19, G28];
   507	- each MPI is 1 to 256 bits, with an exact bit count;
   508	- the body ends exactly after s.
   509	
   510	Type `0x01` (text) is `SIG_FORMAT` (D-10). The codec does not interpret subpacket contents.
   511	
   512	*Reasons.*
   513	- **The legacy header is what the gpg signer emits.** GnuPG 2.4.4 emits exactly this header for a detached
   514	  P-256 signature: `88 75 04 00 13 08 …`, a 119-byte packet, observed 2026-10-12. Its output is therefore `S`
   515	  unchanged.
   516	  - The KMS slot's packet is assembled by Tessera's own code, and it must emit the same form.
   517	  - A tool that emits an OpenPGP-format header must have its output re-framed **before stamping**. The header is
   518	    not covered by the signature hash, so re-framing does not affect validity.
   519	  - A verifier never re-frames.
   520	- **The cap.** 1024 bytes is about 8× a GnuPG signature, and it still bounds the malleable unhashed area.
   521	
   522	**Rule R (raw FIPS 204 / FIPS 205 signature).**
   523	- `S` is the raw signature byte string that the standard's signing algorithm outputs (the FIPS 204 `sigEncode`
   524	  output; the FIPS 205 signature). It has no header and no wrapper.
   525	- Its length is **exactly** the parameter set's signature size. Any other nonzero length gives `SIG_FORMAT`.
   526	- The signed message is the frame itself (`F_manifest` for a possession proof). No trailer, no transformation.
   527	- **Registered pair (branch-registration supplement of 2026-10-16, A9.6).** `KMS-MLDSA65-PURE-GPG-OPENPGP4-ECDSA-P256-SHA256`:
   528	  slot `kms` is ML-DSA-65 (FIPS 204) in pure mode with the empty context, so the FIPS 204 message representative is
   529	  `0x00 ‖ 0x00 ‖ F`; slot `gpg` is rule O. `S_kms` is exactly 3309 bytes. Length 0 gives `SIG_EMPTY`, above 3309
   530	  `LENGTH_CAP`, any other length `SIG_FORMAT`. The codec reads no byte of `S_kms`: a malformed hint encoding or an
   531	  out-of-range `z` is a verification failure (`SIG_INVALID`; `POSSESSION_INVALID` for possession), not a codec code.
   532	- The issuer obtains `S_kms` (attestation and possession) from KMS with `MessageType EXTERNAL_MU` only, sending
   533	  μ = SHAKE256(SHAKE256(pk, 64) ‖ 0x00 ‖ 0x00 ‖ F, 64), pk the raw 1952-byte key (RFC 9881 Appendix D). The result is
   534	  an ordinary pure signature over `F`. The verifier never sees μ and verifies over `F`.
   535	- The kms key under this id is the exact DER SubjectPublicKeyInfo with OID 2.16.840.1.101.3.4.3.18 and no parameters
   536	  (22-byte prefix `308207b2300b0609608648016503040312038207a100`, then the 1952-byte key); anything else is
   537	  `KEY_FORMAT` (RFC 9881 §§2, 4). Its fingerprint is §3.3's `spki-sha256`, unchanged. Key-use assumption (K-R) of
   538	  the supplement replaces PROOF (K) for this slot; the supplement carries both definitions.
   539	- *Consequence to record:* `common.alg` is one value, equal across slots (§0), naming the pair in slot order.
   540	- Vectors: `vectors/hybrid-golden.json` and `vectors/hybrid-reject.json`; agreement run and differential fuzz as in
   541	  PROOF §5 (supplement §8).
   542	- **Branch recorded:** hybrid, 2026-10-16 (A9.6).
   543	
   544	### 5.3 Verification uses the committed bytes, and only those
   545	
   546	The verifier first obtains the ordered tuple `(F_kms, S_kms, F_gpg, S_gpg)`, by either route below. The two are
   547	the same by PROOF Lemma 7.
   548	- decode `A_sig`; or
   549	- encode the presented tuple into `A_sig`.
   550	
   551	It checks that `SHA256(A_sig)` is the digest the OTS evidence attests. It then verifies **each committed `S` over
   552	its committed `F` under its slot's key**, using exactly those bytes.
   553	
   554	The verifier never does any of these:
   555	- verify a separately selected, re-serialized, normalized or reconstructed signature;
   556	- accept a receipt whose presented frames or signatures differ in any byte from the committed tuple
   557	  (`RECEIPT_ANCHOR_MISMATCH`), even when the presented signature is itself valid (negative controls XR-10 to
   558	  XR-14);
   559	- accept a frame-only anchor.
   560	
   561	The vectors exercise the following verification-stage codes. They are reported after every codec code:
   562	- `KEY_MISMATCH`: the presented public key does not hash to its slot's manifest fingerprint;
   563	- `SIG_INVALID`: a committed signature does not verify (XR-16, signatures swapped between slots, anchor rebuilt);
   564	- `UNVERIFIABLE` (`ALG_UNKNOWN`): the algorithm id is well-formed but is not the installation's catalog-supported
   565	  id. Being codec-known (a row of §5.2's table) is not being policy-supported: under the hybrid branch the
   566	  classical id is codec-known and still `UNVERIFIABLE` (V1-PROFILE §1, "Codec-known versus policy-supported").
   567	  [OWNER 2026-10-19, Levadura contested 5] *(Was: "the algorithm id is not registered", which used "registered" in
   568	  two senses.)*
   569	
   570	These codes belong to the signature verification profile (P3), not to P8.
   571	
   572	### 5.4 Issue order (finding 6)
   573	
   574	1. Fix the manifest and the common metadata, including `declared_issue_time`.
   575	2. Build `F_kms` and `F_gpg`.
   576	3. Produce one detached signature per slot over its frame.
   577	4. Verify both signatures over their frames under the slot keys, using the exact bytes to be committed.
   578	5. **Freeze** `S_kms` and `S_gpg`. Any serialization step, such as rule O re-framing, happens before this
   579	   point. Nothing changes a signature byte after it.
   580	6. Build `A_sig` and stamp `SHA256(A_sig)` with OpenTimestamps: **one** stamp per receipt (R-3.42).
   581	7. Obtain and validate the confirmation and time evidence.
   582	8. Ship the complete receipt.
   583	
   584	- A signature never includes its own eventual anchor time or proof.
   585	- A retry that changes any frame or signature byte needs a fresh stamp, and it obeys the existing reissue and
   586	  temporal rules.
   587	- There is no per-signer stamp, and no frame-only proof is kept alongside.
   588	- R-3.9 is clarified, not weakened. Signatures commit to frames, and the receipt anchor commits to frames plus
   589	  signatures.
   590	
   591	## 6. The DNSSEC-snapshot preimage
   592	
   593	```
   594	S = header("tessera-v1/dnssec-snapshot") ‖ u32 len(snapshot) ‖ snapshot        1 ≤ len ≤ 2^24
   595	```
   596	
   597	- The snapshot body's internal format belongs to the profile document (Track 3). P8 fixes only its envelope.
   598	- *Reason:* C4's snapshot rule gives the snapshot its own OTS proof. The tag keeps that proof from being
   599	  presented as a receipt anchor, or the reverse.
   600	
   601	---
   602	
   603	## 7. DNS key-publication record
   604	
   605	C4(i) needs a DNSSEC-authenticated record that binds V1's two keys to D. Neither the spec nor wamason.com has one
   606	yet (spike report; R-4.26 says only "DNSSEC records publishing the fingerprints").
   607	
   608	**Owner name:** `_tessera-v1.<D>`, where D is `common.issuer`.
   609	- The verifier rebuilds this name from the signed `issuer` and compares it, label by label and ignoring ASCII
   610	  case, with the validated RRset's owner name. Anything else gives `DNS_OWNER`.
   611	- *Reason:* the owner name itself is then the C4 owner-name check (G-21/M2). An underscore label cannot collide
   612	  with a hostname, and the `-v1` suffix lets a later profile publish next to it.
   613	
   614	**Type:** TXT. **RRset:** exactly **one** TXT record at the owner name (`DNS_RRSET` if there are zero or more).
   615	**RDATA:** exactly **one** character-string (`DNS_STRINGS` otherwise). If a string's length byte runs past the
   616	RDATA, the result is `DNS_RDATA`.
   617	- *Reason:* one string removes every question about how several strings concatenate. A provider that splits a
   618	  value is non-conforming for V1.
   619	- Every canonical value is **exactly 255 bytes**, the limit for one string (DNS-02): each field has a fixed length
   620	  and, since 2026-10-12, every field is mandatory. That leaves no room for another field. A later field needs a new
   621	  record version.
   622	
   623	**Value grammar** (ABNF; the canonical form is the only accepted form):
   624	
   625	```
   626	value      = "v=tessera1" ";profile=tessera-v1-strict" ";kms=" 64LHEX ";gpg=" 64LHEX
   627	             ";issuance-not-before=" utc ";issuance-not-after=" utc
   628	LHEX       = %x30-39 / %x61-66                              ; lowercase hex only
   629	utc        = 4DIGIT "-" 2DIGIT "-" 2DIGIT "T" 2DIGIT ":" 2DIGIT ":" 2DIGIT "Z"
   630	             ; RFC 3339 UTC; a real Gregorian date and time, seconds 00-59, no fraction
   631	             ; years 0001-9999, proleptic Gregorian [OWNER 2026-10-19, G18]
   632	```
   633	
   634	- `kms` is the hex of §3.3's `spki-sha256`. `gpg` is the hex of §3.3's `gpg-sha256`.
   635	- `issuance-not-before` and `issuance-not-after` are both **mandatory** (since 2026-10-12; `issuance-not-before` was
   636	  optional before). `not-after` must be strictly later than `not-before` (`DNS_WINDOW`), and
   637	  `not-after − not-before` must be at most 90 days, 7 776 000 s (`DNS_WINDOW_LENGTH`; Amendment 9's single V1
   638	  window). §7.1 gives their meaning.
   639	- `utc` is exactly the grammar of `declared_issue_time` (§3.2), so the window and the declared time compare
   640	  without conversion. The record's previous grammar was a date only. A one-second boundary cannot be stated with
   641	  a date, so the window uses the same time grammar as the signed bytes.
   642	- The 2026-10-09 optional `not-before=<date>` is replaced by `issuance-not-before` and is now `DNS_FIELD_UNKNOWN`
   643	  (XD-29).
   644	- The value has no spaces and no trailing `;`. Field order is fixed. Bytes are printable ASCII from 0x21 to 0x7E.
   645	
   646	Example (DNS-02):
   647	
   648	```
   649	v=tessera1;profile=tessera-v1-strict;kms=5cd252fb…7cd3;gpg=5ecd7121…8fc3;issuance-not-before=2026-10-12T00:00:00Z;issuance-not-after=2027-01-10T00:00:00Z
   650	```
   651	
   652	**Rejection order** (the first that applies):
   653	1. `DNS_OWNER`
   654	2. `DNS_RRSET`
   655	3. `DNS_RDATA`
   656	4. `DNS_STRINGS`
   657	5. `DNS_SYNTAX`:
   658	   - a byte outside 0x21–0x7E;
   659	   - a field that is not `key=value`, with key `[a-z][a-z-]*` and a nonempty value containing no `=`;
   660	   - an empty field.
   661	6. `DNS_VERSION`: the first field is not `v=tessera1`, or `v` appears again.
   662	7. `DNS_FIELD_UNKNOWN`: an extra field.
   663	8. `DNS_SLOT_DUPLICATE` (`kms` or `gpg` appears twice) or `DNS_FIELD_DUPLICATE` (another field appears twice).
   664	9. `DNS_SLOT_MISSING`.
   665	10. `DNS_FIELD_MISSING` (`profile`, `issuance-not-before` or `issuance-not-after`).
   666	11. `DNS_ORDER`.
   667	12. `DNS_PROFILE`.
   668	13. `DNS_VALUE`: the hex or a time fails its grammar.
   669	14. `DNS_WINDOW`: `issuance-not-after` ≤ `issuance-not-before`.
   670	15. `DNS_WINDOW_LENGTH`: `issuance-not-after − issuance-not-before` > 90 days (7 776 000 s).
   671	
   672	**Why the record is unambiguous** (outside PROOF.md's frame domain):
   673	- The accepted values form a regular language. Every field begins with a fixed literal key, and every value
   674	  comes from an alphabet without `;` or `=`, so splitting on `;` and then on the first `=` recovers the fields
   675	  uniquely.
   676	- The field order is fixed, and every value has exactly one spelling: lowercase hex, and `YYYY-MM-DDThh:mm:ssZ`.
   677	  So the map from (kms, gpg, issuance-not-before, issuance-not-after) to value strings is injective, and its
   678	  inverse is the parse. `:` is outside the separators, and `DNS_WINDOW` and `DNS_WINDOW_LENGTH` are checks on parsed
   679	  values, so none of them affects the split.
   680	- One RR with one character-string removes concatenation ambiguity. The RDATA length byte delimits the string.
   681	- The owner name is compared as a label sequence, so `_tessera-v1.wamason.com` cannot match
   682	  `_tessera-v1.wamason.com.evil.example` or `tessera-v1.wamason.com` (XD-12 to XD-14).
   683	- The value begins `v=` (0x76), while every tagged string in §2 begins with a byte from 0x11 to 0x1d. So a TXT
   684	  value can never be a V1 frame, and a frame can never be a TXT value.
   685	
   686	### 7.1 Pre-committed issuance window (owner decision 2026-10-12; single 90-day window per Amendment 9)
   687	
   688	The key-publication record carries the issuance window. For V1 there is one window, of at most 90 days
   689	(`DNS_WINDOW_LENGTH`), with no overlapping successor: before it ends, either a rotation mechanism is registered or
   690	issuance stops (Amendment 9, A9.6). It is the same canonical value in both channels: the DNS
   691	TXT record, and the git key-assertion blob (§7.2). The same window value is authenticated by the anchored git
   692	assertion and by DNSSEC at the snapshot's own anchored time. Its pre-commitment is **chain order**: the git
   693	assertion's selected anchor height plus 5 is lower than the receipt's selected anchor height, so the window was
   694	confirmed at k = 6 in the chain before the receipt was anchored (A9 C4; V1-PROFILE §6.3, `GIT_NOT_PRECOMMITTED`).
   695	That is chain order, not UTC (A2.1), and DNSSEC evidence establishes no publication before issue (A9 C4). [OWNER
   696	2026-10-19, G02 CORRECT; Levadura C1] *(Was: "The window is therefore fixed in DNSSEC-authenticated and anchored
   697	authority evidence before any receipt that relies on it", which overstated both channels.)*
   698	
   699	**Verifier rule (C4).** A receipt passes C4 only if both of these hold:
   700	- `issuance-not-before` ≤ `declared_issue_time`;
   701	- **T ≤ `issuance-not-after`**, where T is the header time of the qualifying attestation at the **lowest block
   702	  height** in the presented OTS proof (A9 C5; A10.5's stages; V1-PROFILE §7.1). Header times are not monotone, so
   703	  selection is by height, never by least header time. [OWNER 2026-10-19, r2-contra; Levadura contested 1] *(Was:
   704	  "the time of the earliest verified attestation", A9's pre-2026-10-14 wording.)*
   705	
   706	Otherwise the verdict is `INVALID`, with reason `ISSUANCE_WINDOW`. Both bounds are inclusive: W-01 is
   707	T = not-after, W-02 is declared time = not-before. XW-01 is T = not-after + 1 s, and XW-02 is a declared time
   708	1 s early.
   709	
   710	*Rationale, recorded.*
   711	- **A surviving or stolen key cannot mint a receipt that passes with T after the window.** Keys may outlive the
   712	  service's exit, or be stolen. Even signing capability for every required slot cannot produce a receipt that
   713	  passes with verified T after `issuance-not-after`, under continuing anchor, history and authority assumptions. A fresh mint's `A_sig` (§5) can only
   714	  be stamped at or after the moment it exists, and the anchor prevents claiming an earlier T. So every receipt
   715	  whose verified T is after the window fails (XW-03). This is a statement in chain time, not in UTC (see the
   716	  residual below).
   717	  - A compromise *before* `not-after` is not covered. Within the window, a thief mints receipts that pass, subject
   718	    to the historical-security caveats of CODEX-REVIEW findings 7 and 8.
   719	- **Key-format expiries are not relied on.**
   720	  - OpenPGP key-expiration subpackets live in a self-signature. Any keyholder, a thief included, can re-issue that
   721	    self-signature to extend them.
   722	  - A raw SPKI key, such as a KMS ML-DSA key, carries no expiry at all.
   723	  - The window is instead fixed in authority evidence that the keyholder alone cannot rewrite after the fact.
   724	    Rewriting it means re-publishing the record, which is a new authority event with its own evidence times.
   725	
   726	*Residual: chain time (A2.1).*
   727	- T is the **header-time-derived T as registered**. The verifier compares it with `issuance-not-after` directly,
   728	  with no margin added or subtracted. That keeps one registered meaning of T.
   729	- A Bitcoin header's timestamp is not an exact UTC clock. Consensus constrains it only loosely: it must exceed the
   730	  median of the previous eleven blocks, and nodes accept it up to about two hours ahead of their own clock.
   731	- In the direction that matters here, a stamp actually made **after** `not-after` can carry a header time at or
   732	  before `not-after`. Its receipt passes. A2.1 registers no finite bound on that backward skew, and neither ε nor S
   733	  is one. So what this rule guarantees is "no receipt passes with verified T after `not-after`". Translating that
   734	  into a finite UTC cutoff needs an additional, explicit backward-skew assumption that V1 does not establish
   735	  (Codex review of Amendment 9, finding 2; an earlier draft of this paragraph claimed such a bound).
   736	- The opposite error, an honest stamp made just before `not-after` getting a later header time and failing, is
   737	  the safe direction.
   738	- *Issuer invariant:* an attempt whose T would exceed `not-after` is not shipped. The stop margin that implements
   739	  it is an operating number, not part of this encoding (`docs/V1-OPERATIONS-RUNBOOK.md`). The verdict reports the
   740	  comparison with T as registered.
   741	
   742	### 7.2 Git channel: what its OTS stamp must cover (finding 9; added 2026-10-12)
   743	
   744	- **The blob's content.** Since the issuance-window decision (§7.1), the git key-assertion blob carries the same
   745	  canonical value as the TXT record, so both channels assert one byte string, window included.
   746	- **What P8 does not fix.** The blob's path, and any byte framing around the value: for example, whether a
   747	  trailing LF is allowed. Those are not P8's.
   748	- **What P8 fixes here.** The blob's anchor target.
   749	
   750	- **The stamp targets the signed commit's object id.** That commit's tree contains the assertion blob at the
   751	  profile path (V1-PROFILE §6.3, `tessera-v1/key-assertion`), and it carries a `gpgsig` header that verifies under
   752	  the repository key. Whether this commit introduced the blob is not checked (V1-PROFILE §15.4). [OWNER 2026-10-19,
   753	  r2-contra; Levadura C4] *(Was: "That commit adds the assertion blob".)*
   754	- These stamp targets are **not acceptable**:
   755	  - the tree;
   756	  - the blob;
   757	  - an unsigned commit, including the unsigned payload that the commit signature covers.
   758	- *Why, from git's documented object format:*
   759	  - gitformat-signature(5) (git 2.43.0, "COMMIT SIGNATURES") gives a commit signature's payload as the commit
   760	    object, embedded as the header entry `gpgsig`.
   761	  - gitglossary(7) says an object "is uniquely identified by the SHA-1 of its contents".
   762	  - So a signed commit's id is computed over the object **including** its `gpgsig` header. The signature lies
   763	    inside the anchored bytes.
   764	  - A stamp over the tree or blob covers no signature. A stamp over the unsigned commit is the receipt's own
   765	    pre-stage-then-sign gap again.
   766	- *Checked 2026-10-12* (git 2.43.0, GnuPG 2.4.4, scratch repository):
   767	  - the signed commit's id equals `SHA-1("commit " ‖ decimal size ‖ 0x00 ‖ raw object)`;
   768	  - the raw object contains the `gpgsig` header;
   769	  - `git hash-object -t commit` reproduces the id;
   770	  - the unsigned commit with the same tree had a different id, while its tree id was identical.
   771	- **Matching the repository's convention.** This repository stamps a file `timestamps/<id>` holding the commit's
   772	  lowercase hex id and LF, after the commit exists. For a signed commit, that is this target.
   773	- **What the verifier does.** It:
   774	  1. recomputes the id from the presented raw commit object;
   775	  2. verifies `gpgsig` over the object without that header;
   776	  3. follows the commit to its tree and then to the blob;
   777	  4. checks that the stamped bytes name that id.
   778	- The git signature is ASCII-armored inside the commit header. §5.2's binary rule applies to receipt slots only.
   779	- **Residual, already recorded.** Two parts are SHA-1:
   780	  - the commit id itself;
   781	  - the commit → tree → blob linkage.
   782	
   783	  Both are in the dependency manifest (V1-DERIVATION's bundle contents and custody residual) and outside P8's
   784	  SHA-256 scope (PROOF (H)). Anchoring a signed commit puts its signature inside the anchored bytes. It does not
   785	  turn the SHA-1 links into SHA-256 bindings.
