   110	
   111	## 1. Canonical payload bytes: RFC 8785 over the accepted domain
   112	
   113	### 1.1 The accepted value domain V
   114	
   115	A JSON value is in V if and only if all of the following hold.
   116	- **Literals.** `null`, `true` and `false`.
   117	- **Numbers.** A finite IEEE 754 binary64 value other than −0. *Reason: this is exactly what RFC 8785 can
   118	  serialize without loss.*
   119	- **Strings, including member names.** A sequence of Unicode scalar values with:
   120	  - no surrogate code point (U+D800 to U+DFFF), and so no lone `\uD8xx` escape;
   121	  - no noncharacter (U+FDD0 to U+FDEF, and U+xFFFE or U+xFFFF in every plane). *Reason: RFC 7493 §2.1 (I-JSON); G-04.*
   122	  - no code point that is unassigned (General_Category `Cn`) in **Unicode 16.0.0**. *Reason: this pins the NFC test
   123	    (next item) to one table. Under Unicode's stability policy, a string built from characters assigned in 16.0.0
   124	    that is NFC under 16.0.0 stays NFC under every later version. An unassigned code point carries no such
   125	    guarantee, so two verifiers on different Unicode versions could disagree about it.*
   126	  - the string already in NFC. It is **rejected, never normalized**. *Reason: R-3.4, "inputs relying on Unicode
   127	    normalization assumptions"; G-05 kept.*
   128	- **Arrays.** Arrays of members of V.
   129	- **Objects.** Objects with pairwise distinct member names, compared after unescaping. *Reason: R-3.4's duplicate
   130	  names; G-04.*
   131	- **Depth.** Nesting depth is at most **64** for a whole payload. Each array or object adds 1. The requester's
   132	  `content` sits at depth 3, so it may nest at most **62** levels. *Reason: this pins a limit that real parsers
   133	  otherwise set differently.*
   134	- **Size.** A payload is at most **2^24 bytes** (16 MiB). *Reason: a fixed cap that both implementations
   135	  enforce. No V1 payload comes near it.*
   136	
   137	### 1.2 The number rule: value-preserving (G-03 and G-56 resolved for V1)
   138	
   139	A number **literal** `L` (RFC 8259 grammar) in submitted JSON text is accepted if and only if:
   140	1. L is not a negative zero: no `-` with zero value (`-0`, `-0.0`, `-0e5`). *Reason: the R-3.4 list.*
   141	2. `d` = the binary64 nearest to L's exact decimal value, under round-half-even, is finite. Overflow is rejected.
   142	3. **The exact decimal value of `ES(d)` equals the exact decimal value of L.** `ES` is ECMAScript
   143	   Number::toString, the serialization RFC 8785 §3.2.2.3 uses.
   144	
   145	Accepted: `9.8`, `9.80`, `1E2`, `1e21`, `5e-324`, `123456789012345680000` and `0e99999999999999999999`. Each keeps its
   146	value, and only the spelling changes.
   147	
   148	Rejected, each with its reason:
   149	- `1e400`: overflow.
   150	- `1e-400`: nonzero, and rounds to 0.
   151	- `333333333.33333329` and `9007199254740993`: the value would change.
   152	- `0.1000000000000000055511151231257827021181583404541015625`: this is *exactly* a double, but RFC 8785 prints it as
   153	  `0.1`, which has a different value.
   154	- `4.9e-324`: rounds to the smallest subnormal, which prints as `5e-324`.
   155	- `-0`.
   156	- `NaN` and `Infinity`: not JSON at all.
   157	
   158	Subnormals that survive rule 3 are accepted, so G-03b is reversed: RFC 8785 treats them as ordinary.
   159	
   160	**Why this rule and not the registered literal rule.** Read literally, "exactly representable as a double" does
   161	not compose with RFC 8785. It *accepts* `0.1000000000000000055511151231257827021181583404541015625`, whose
   162	canonical form `0.1` it then *rejects* when the verifier re-reads the payload. So under that reading,
   163	`canonical()` maps its own domain outside itself. The value-preserving rule is the largest rule under which:
   164	- (a) canonicalization never changes a value; and
   165	- (b) every canonical output is itself accepted.
   166	**This changes A1 P8's rejection item and prereg §4.3's wording. A9.8 registers it for `tessera-v1-strict`,
   167	replacing both (A9.7 rows 22–23).** [OWNER 2026-10-19, G38] *(Was: "… and it needs an author sentence (§9, D-1). The
   168	V1 derivation's profile line, 'registered number rule unchanged', must be reconciled with it either way." A9.8
   169	supplied the sentence, and the derivation's line now states the value-preserving rule.)*
   170	
   171	What "never helpfully normalized" means here. The **issuer's ingestion** normalizes *spelling only*:
   172	- insignificant whitespace;
   173	- member order;
   174	- escape choice;
   175	- number spelling.
   176	
   177	It never changes a value. The **verifier normalizes nothing**: a payload must already equal `JCS` of its own
   178	parse (§1.4, `JSON_NONCANONICAL`). Injectivity (PROOF.md) is stated over *values*. Two texts that differ only in
   179	spelling are one value by definition, and they get one canonical byte string. That is intended.
   180	
   181	### 1.3 Serialization
   182	
   183	`canonical(v) = JCS(v)` (RFC 8785):
   184	- UTF-8 output, with no insignificant whitespace;
   185	- object members sorted by the UTF-16 code units of their names;
   186	- numbers printed by `ES`;
   187	- strings escaped only for `"`, `\` and U+0000 to U+001F: `\b \t \n \f \r` where defined, `\u00xx` with
   188	  lowercase hex otherwise. Every other scalar appears literally.
   189	
   190	*Reason: R-3.1 binds to RFC 8785. Vectors C-04 and C-05 pin the escape and the UTF-16 ordering traps, for example
   191	U+1F600 sorting before U+E000.*
   192	
   193	### 1.4 Decoding, and the order in which rejections are reported
   194	
   195	Two entry points.
   196	- `ingest(text)` takes requester JSON: whitespace is allowed, and the depth limit is 62.
   197	- `payload(bytes)` takes in-frame bytes: the depth limit is 64, and the bytes must be canonical.
   198	
   199	Each runs these checks in this order and reports the **first category that fails**, scanning the whole value for
   200	each category. *Reason: both implementations then agree on the reason code, not only on rejection.*
   201	
   202	1. `JSON_ENCODING`: the bytes are not strict UTF-8. Overlongs, encoded surrogates and values above U+10FFFF are
   203	   rejected.
   204	2. `JSON_SYNTAX`:
   205	   - a BOM; not exactly one RFC 8259 JSON text; trailing non-whitespace;
   206	   - a raw control character in a string; bad escapes; leading zeros; `+`; `.5`; `1.`; `NaN`; `Infinity`.
   207	3. `JSON_DEPTH`.
   208	4. `JSON_DUPLICATE_NAME`.
   209	5. `JSON_STRING`: a surrogate, a noncharacter, an unassigned code point, or text that is not NFC.
   210	6. `JSON_NUMBER`: §1.2.
   211	7. *(`payload` only)* `JSON_NONCANONICAL`: the bytes are not equal to `JCS` of the parsed value.
   212	
   213	---
