   110	
   111	## 1. Canonical payload bytes: RFC 8785 over the accepted domain
   112	
   113	### 1.1 The accepted value domain V
   114	
   115	A JSON value is in V if and only if all of the following hold.
   116	- **Literals.** `null`, `true` and `false`.
   117	- **Numbers.** A finite IEEE 754 binary64 value other than −0. *Reason: this is exactly what RFC 8785 can
   118	  serialize without loss.*
   119	- **Strings, including member names.** A sequence of Unicode scalar values with:
   120	  - no surrogate code point (U+D800 to U+DFFF), and so no lone `\uD8xx` escape;
   121	  - no noncharacter (U+FDD0 to U+FDEF, and U+xFFFE or U+xFFFF in every plane). *Reason: RFC 7493 §2.1 (I-JSON); G-04.*
   122	  - no code point that is unassigned (General_Category `Cn`) in **Unicode 16.0.0**. *Reason: this pins the NFC test
   123	    (next item) to one table. Under Unicode's stability policy, a string built from characters assigned in 16.0.0
   124	    that is NFC under 16.0.0 stays NFC under every later version. An unassigned code point carries no such
   125	    guarantee, so two verifiers on different Unicode versions could disagree about it.*
   126	  - the string already in NFC. It is **rejected, never normalized**. *Reason: R-3.4, "inputs relying on Unicode
   127	    normalization assumptions"; G-05 kept.*
   128	- **Arrays.** Arrays of members of V.
   129	- **Objects.** Objects with pairwise distinct member names, compared after unescaping. *Reason: R-3.4's duplicate
   130	  names; G-04.*
   131	- **Depth.** Nesting depth is at most **64** for a whole payload. Each array or object adds 1. The requester's
   132	  `content` sits at depth 3, so it may nest at most **62** levels. *Reason: this pins a limit that real parsers
   133	  otherwise set differently.*
   134	- **Size.** A payload is at most **2^24 bytes** (16 MiB). *Reason: a fixed cap that both implementations
   135	  enforce. No V1 payload comes near it.*
   136	
   137	### 1.2 The number rule: value-preserving (G-03 and G-56 resolved for V1)
   138	
   139	A number **literal** `L` (RFC 8259 grammar) in submitted JSON text is accepted if and only if:
   140	1. L is not a negative zero: no `-` with zero value (`-0`, `-0.0`, `-0e5`). *Reason: the R-3.4 list.*
   141	2. `d` = the binary64 nearest to L's exact decimal value, under round-half-even, is finite. Overflow is rejected.
   142	3. **The exact decimal value of `ES(d)` equals the exact decimal value of L.** `ES` is ECMAScript
   143	   Number::toString, the serialization RFC 8785 §3.2.2.3 uses.
   144	
   145	Accepted: `9.8`, `9.80`, `1E2`, `1e21`, `5e-324`, `123456789012345680000` and `0e99999999999999999999`. Each keeps its
   146	value, and only the spelling changes.
   147	
   148	Rejected, each with its reason:
   149	- `1e400`: overflow.
   150	- `1e-400`: nonzero, and rounds to 0.
   151	- `333333333.33333329` and `9007199254740993`: the value would change.
   152	- `0.1000000000000000055511151231257827021181583404541015625`: this is *exactly* a double, but RFC 8785 prints it as
   153	  `0.1`, which has a different value.
   154	- `4.9e-324`: rounds to the smallest subnormal, which prints as `5e-324`.
   155	- `-0`.
   156	- `NaN` and `Infinity`: not JSON at all.
   157	
   158	Subnormals that survive rule 3 are accepted, so G-03b is reversed: RFC 8785 treats them as ordinary.
   159	
   160	**Why this rule and not the registered literal rule.** Read literally, "exactly representable as a double" does
   161	not compose with RFC 8785. It *accepts* `0.1000000000000000055511151231257827021181583404541015625`, whose
   162	canonical form `0.1` it then *rejects* when the verifier re-reads the payload. So under that reading,
   163	`canonical()` maps its own domain outside itself. The value-preserving rule is the largest rule under which:
   164	- (a) canonicalization never changes a value; and
   165	- (b) every canonical output is itself accepted.
   166	**This changes A1 P8's rejection item and prereg §4.3's wording. A9.8 registers it for `tessera-v1-strict`,
   167	replacing both (A9.7 rows 22–23).** [OWNER 2026-10-19, G38] *(Was: "… and it needs an author sentence (§9, D-1). The
   168	V1 derivation's profile line, 'registered number rule unchanged', must be reconciled with it either way." A9.8
   169	supplied the sentence, and the derivation's line now states the value-preserving rule.)*
   170	
   171	What "never helpfully normalized" means here. The **issuer's ingestion** normalizes *spelling only*:
   172	- insignificant whitespace;
   173	- member order;
   174	- escape choice;
   175	- number spelling.
   176	
   177	It never changes a value. The **verifier normalizes nothing**: a payload must already equal `JCS` of its own
   178	parse (§1.4, `JSON_NONCANONICAL`). Injectivity (PROOF.md) is stated over *values*. Two texts that differ only in
   179	spelling are one value by definition, and they get one canonical byte string. That is intended.
   180	
   181	### 1.3 Serialization
   182	
   183	`canonical(v) = JCS(v)` (RFC 8785):
   184	- UTF-8 output, with no insignificant whitespace;
   185	- object members sorted by the UTF-16 code units of their names;
   186	- numbers printed by `ES`;
   187	- strings escaped only for `"`, `\` and U+0000 to U+001F: `\b \t \n \f \r` where defined, `\u00xx` with
   188	  lowercase hex otherwise. Every other scalar appears literally.
   189	
   190	*Reason: R-3.1 binds to RFC 8785. Vectors C-04 and C-05 pin the escape and the UTF-16 ordering traps, for example
   191	U+1F600 sorting before U+E000.*
   192	
   193	### 1.4 Decoding, and the order in which rejections are reported
   194	
   195	Two entry points.
   196	- `ingest(text)` takes requester JSON: whitespace is allowed, and the depth limit is 62.
   197	- `payload(bytes)` takes in-frame bytes: the depth limit is 64, and the bytes must be canonical.
   198	
   199	Each runs these checks in this order and reports the **first category that fails**, scanning the whole value for
   200	each category. *Reason: both implementations then agree on the reason code, not only on rejection.*
   201	
   202	1. `JSON_ENCODING`: the bytes are not strict UTF-8. Overlongs, encoded surrogates and values above U+10FFFF are
   203	   rejected.
   204	2. `JSON_SYNTAX`:
   205	   - a BOM; not exactly one RFC 8259 JSON text; trailing non-whitespace;
   206	   - a raw control character in a string; bad escapes; leading zeros; `+`; `.5`; `1.`; `NaN`; `Infinity`.
   207	3. `JSON_DEPTH`.
   208	4. `JSON_DUPLICATE_NAME`.
   209	5. `JSON_STRING`: a surrogate, a noncharacter, an unassigned code point, or text that is not NFC.
   210	6. `JSON_NUMBER`: §1.2.
   211	7. *(`payload` only)* `JSON_NONCANONICAL`: the bytes are not equal to `JCS` of the parsed value.
   212	
   213	---
   214	
   215	## 2. The tagged byte strings: one header, four tags
   216	
   217	Every byte string that a V1 slot key signs (attestation and manifest frames), and every receipt-anchor and snapshot
   218	preimage that OTS stamps, begins with the same header. Two V1 byte strings carry no header: the git channel's
   219	stamped message, which is exactly the 40 lowercase hex digits of the signed commit id followed by one LF (A9.5,
   220	§7.2), and the key-publication value (§7), which the repository key and the DNSSEC keys sign through git and DNS.
   221	Neither is a frame (Remark 10 of PROOF.md). [OWNER 2026-10-19, Levadura C6] *(Was: "Every V1 byte string that a V1
   222	key signs or that OTS stamps begins with the same header:", which the git stamped message contradicts.)*
   223	
   224	The header:
   225	
   226	```
   227	header(t) = u8 len(t) || t                       t ∈ T (ASCII, no NUL)
   228	```
   229	
   230	| Tag `t` | len | First bytes (hex) | Construction |
   231	|---|---|---|---|
   232	| `tessera-v1/attest` | 17 | `11 74 65 73 …` | frame, §3 |
   233	| `tessera-v1/manifest` | 19 | `13 74 65 73 …` | frame, §4 |
   234	| `tessera-v1/receipt-anchor-sig` | 29 | `1d 74 65 73 …` | signature-inclusive anchor preimage `A_sig`, §5 |
   235	| `tessera-v1/dnssec-snapshot` | 26 | `1a 74 65 73 …` | snapshot preimage, §6 |
   236	
   237	`tessera-v1/receipt-anchor` (25, `19 …`), the frame-only anchor tag of the 2026-10-09 draft, is **withdrawn**. It
   238	is not in T, so it is rejected with `TAG_UNKNOWN` (§5.1).
   239	
   240	- One string serves as both the domain-separation tag and the object type. R-3.11 lists the two as separate items,
   241	  but A1 P8's four-field frame has a single slot. The prefix `tessera-v1/` does the domain separation, and the
   242	  suffix names the type (G-07 resolved).
   243	- A tag outside T is rejected with `TAG_UNKNOWN`. That includes P7 types V1 does not use (`tessera-v1/wrapper`)
   244	  and later versions (`tessera-v2/attest`). A tag in T that is not the expected one is rejected with
   356	## 4. Theorem (P8 for `tessera-v1-strict`)
   357	
   358	Let the accepted sets be as in §1.
   359	
   360	- **(i) Injectivity.** These maps are injective:
   361	  - `canonical = JCS` on V;
   362	  - `frame(t, ·)` on `Pay(t)`, and so `v ↦ frame(t, JCS(v))` on the schema-valid values;
   363	  - `A` on ordered (frame, signature) tuples `(F_kms, S_kms, F_gpg, S_gpg)` (Lemmas 7, 7a);
   364	  - `S` on snapshot bodies.
   365	
   366	  Each decoder is the exact inverse of its encoder on the accepted set: `Dec ∘ Enc = id` on accepted structures,
   367	  and `Enc ∘ Dec = id` on accepted bytes. *(Lemmas 3, 5, 7, 7a, 8.)*
   368	
   369	  So the map from an accepted receipt's structure `(M, C, S_kms, S_gpg)` to `(F_M, F_kms, F_gpg, A_sig)` is
   370	  injective outright:
   371	  - `F_M` determines `M`;
   372	  - `F_kms` determines `C`;
   373	  - `A_sig` determines `S_kms` and `S_gpg`, byte for byte.
   374	
   375	  The receipt is not determined by `(M, C)` alone (§1).
   376	
   377	- **(ii) Boundary-unambiguity.** In every accepted byte string, each field's extent is a function of the bytes
   378	  before it. The end of input is required to fall exactly where the last length prefix says. The JSON payload is
   379	  delimited by the frame's length, never by its own syntax. A signature's extent is fixed by its slot length, and
   380	  it must agree with the signature's own extent. *(Lemmas 5, 7, 7a, 8; Lemma 9 for the OpenPGP signed data.)*
   381	
   382	- **(iii) Type and domain separation.** The accepted sets `Frm(attest)`, `Frm(manifest)`, `Anc` and `Snp` are
   383	  pairwise disjoint (Lemma 4). A decoder for one construction rejects every member of the others (`TAG_MISMATCH`).
   384	  The withdrawn frame-only anchor layout is rejected by every decoder (`TAG_UNKNOWN`; Lemma 7's corollary).
   385	
   386	  Inside `Frm(attest)`, the `kms` and `gpg` slot frames are disjoint, each frame's slot is fixed by its own bytes,
   387	  and an accepted receipt's two frames are distinct and byte-identical on `JCS(C)` (Lemma 6).
   388	
   389	  The DNS value and foreign formats are disjoint from all of these (Remark 10).
   390	
   391	- **(iv) Computational binding, under (H)** *(revised 2026-10-12)*. Each of the following binds only under SHA-256
   392	  collision resistance.
   393	  - A frame's signed bytes determine the manifest frame, the specification, the conformance vectors and the
   394	    preamble, through their SHA-256 values.
   395	  - The OTS-stamped digest `SHA256(A_sig)` determines the ordered tuple `((F_kms, S_kms), (F_gpg, S_gpg))`,
   396	    through Lemma 7: the frames **and the exact signature bytes**, in slot order.
   397	
   398	  So two different such objects bound by one accepted frame or anchor digest yield a SHA-256 collision. This is
   399	  the only place P8 for V1 rests on a cryptographic assumption.
   400	
   401	  **Four statements, kept apart** (finding 10). P8 proves (a) and (b). (c) and (d) are not P8's, and P8 does not
   402	  claim them.
   403	  - **(a) Codec injectivity** (unconditional). `A_sig` decodes to exactly one tuple and is re-encoded from it
   404	    (Lemma 7). A different signature byte string, even a valid one over the same frame, gives a different
   405	    preimage.
   406	  - **(b) Digest binding** (under (H)). A digest determines that tuple. A second tuple with the same digest is a
   407	    SHA-256 collision.
   408	  - **(c) Unforgeability** (P1 and P3, not P8). Whether `S` verifies over `F` under the slot key is a separate
   409	    check. It is made on the committed bytes (E§5.3). Whether a verifying `S` was produced by the key holder
   410	    rests on the signature scheme's security and on key custody.
   411	  - **(d) Temporal composition** (C5, not P8). Suppose valid OTS and Bitcoin evidence shows that
   412	    `SHA256(A_sig)` existed by time T. Then by (b), the exact bytes `S_kms` and `S_gpg` existed by T, under that
   413	    evidence's own chain-time residuals.
   414	    - Hence a signature first produced after T, for example once a key is stolen or the algorithm broken, cannot
   415	      be substituted into this receipt while (H) and the temporal evidence hold.
   416	    - It does **not** follow that the signatures were produced honestly before T. That needs the historical
   417	      signing, custody and authority assumptions of CODEX-REVIEW findings 7 and 8, which V1 reports as not
   418	      evaluated.
   419	
   420	∎ (from the lemmas)
   421	
   422	**What is not claimed.** P8 does not claim any of the following:
   423	- that signatures are unforgeable, or that keys are bound to identities (P1, P3);
   424	- that a stamp's time bound makes signing honest (iv)(d);
   425	- that the values have any meaning (A3 §A3.1);
   426	- that any value outside V is accepted. It is rejected with the code its first failing check names.
