   742	### 7.2 Git channel: what its OTS stamp must cover (finding 9; added 2026-10-12)
   743	
   744	- **The blob's content.** Since the issuance-window decision (§7.1), the git key-assertion blob carries the same
   745	  canonical value as the TXT record, so both channels assert one byte string, window included.
   746	- **What P8 does not fix.** The blob's path, and any byte framing around the value: for example, whether a
   747	  trailing LF is allowed. Those are not P8's.
   748	- **What P8 fixes here.** The blob's anchor target.
   749	
   750	- **The stamp targets the signed commit's object id.** That commit's tree contains the assertion blob at the
   751	  profile path (V1-PROFILE §6.3, `tessera-v1/key-assertion`), and it carries a `gpgsig` header that verifies under
   752	  the repository key. Whether this commit introduced the blob is not checked (V1-PROFILE §15.4). [OWNER 2026-10-19,
   753	  r2-contra; Levadura C4] *(Was: "That commit adds the assertion blob".)*
   754	- These stamp targets are **not acceptable**:
   755	  - the tree;
   756	  - the blob;
   757	  - an unsigned commit, including the unsigned payload that the commit signature covers.
   758	- *Why, from git's documented object format:*
   759	  - gitformat-signature(5) (git 2.43.0, "COMMIT SIGNATURES") gives a commit signature's payload as the commit
   760	    object, embedded as the header entry `gpgsig`.
   761	  - gitglossary(7) says an object "is uniquely identified by the SHA-1 of its contents".
   762	  - So a signed commit's id is computed over the object **including** its `gpgsig` header. The signature lies
   763	    inside the anchored bytes.
   764	  - A stamp over the tree or blob covers no signature. A stamp over the unsigned commit is the receipt's own
   765	    pre-stage-then-sign gap again.
   766	- *Checked 2026-10-12* (git 2.43.0, GnuPG 2.4.4, scratch repository):
   767	  - the signed commit's id equals `SHA-1("commit " ‖ decimal size ‖ 0x00 ‖ raw object)`;
   768	  - the raw object contains the `gpgsig` header;
   769	  - `git hash-object -t commit` reproduces the id;
   770	  - the unsigned commit with the same tree had a different id, while its tree id was identical.
   771	- **Matching the repository's convention.** This repository stamps a file `timestamps/<id>` holding the commit's
   772	  lowercase hex id and LF, after the commit exists. For a signed commit, that is this target.
   773	- **What the verifier does.** It:
   774	  1. recomputes the id from the presented raw commit object;
   775	  2. verifies `gpgsig` over the object without that header;
   776	  3. follows the commit to its tree and then to the blob;
   777	  4. checks that the stamped bytes name that id.
   778	- The git signature is ASCII-armored inside the commit header. §5.2's binary rule applies to receipt slots only.
   779	- **Residual, already recorded.** Two parts are SHA-1:
   780	  - the commit id itself;
   781	  - the commit → tree → blob linkage.
   782	
   783	  Both are in the dependency manifest (V1-DERIVATION's bundle contents and custody residual) and outside P8's
   784	  SHA-256 scope (PROOF (H)). Anchoring a signed commit puts its signature inside the anchored bytes. It does not
   785	  turn the SHA-1 links into SHA-256 bindings.
   786	
   787	---
   788	
   789	## 8. Identifiers and versions, collected
   790	
   791	| Identifier | Encoding | Where |
   792	|---|---|---|
   793	| Construction / object type | `tessera-v1/{attest,manifest,receipt-anchor-sig,dnssec-snapshot}` | §2 header |
   794	| Canonicalization version | u16 `0x0001` | frame offset 18 |
   795	| Profile | `"tessera-v1-strict"` | `common.profile`; DNS `profile=` |
   796	| Verification spec | `common.spec.id` + `common.spec.sha256` | §3.2 |
   797	| Authority statement version | `"map-v1"` | manifest `ver` |
   798	| DNS record version | `v=tessera1` | §7 |
   799	| Algorithm | `common.alg` | §3.2 |
   800	| Signature-slot rule | `(common.alg, slot)` | §5.2 |
   801	
   802	**Identifier rule (finding 5).**
   803	- No identifier in this table, and no semantic, standing or attempt identifier anywhere in V1, is derived from
   804	  signature bytes.
   805	- The receipt-anchor digest `SHA256(A_sig)` commits to the signature bytes, by design. It is an existence
   806	  commitment, not an identifier (§3.5).
   807	
   808	**Dispatch boundary (O-78, an owner decision):**
   809	- The verifier dispatches on the bundle's **unsigned** profile label. An unrecognized label gives
   810	  `UNVERIFIABLE / OUT_OF_PROFILE`, and nothing in this document is evaluated.
   811	- Once a bundle is dispatched to `tessera-v1-strict`, every rejection here is `INVALID`. That includes a signed
   812	  `profile` other than `tessera-v1-strict`, an unknown tag and an unknown canonicalization version, because the
   813	  bundle claimed V1 and its signed bytes contradict that claim.
   814	- The single **codec-level** in-profile `UNVERIFIABLE` that concerns these bytes is a well-formed unknown `alg`
   815	  (stage (c)), and P8 lets that through. The profile adds further in-profile `UNVERIFIABLE` outcomes on these bytes
   816	  (for example `BINDING_FORM_UNSUPPORTED` and `SPEC_UNKNOWN`; V1-PROFILE §3.1 item 5). [OWNER 2026-10-19, G01;
   817	  Levadura C5] *(Was: "The single in-profile `UNVERIFIABLE` …".)*
   818	
   819	## 9. Owner decisions taken here, for the author's record
   820	
   821	D-1 **Number rule: value-preserving (§1.2).** This changes A1 P8's item "numbers not exactly representable as
   822	IEEE 754 doubles" and prereg §4.3's "must be exactly representable". **A9.8 registers it** (A9.7 rows 22–23), as
   823	Codex review 14 asked. The registered wording also fails to compose with RFC 8785 (§1.2). [OWNER 2026-10-19, G38]
   824	*(Was: "It needs an A9 sentence. … The derivation's profile line, which says the rule is unchanged, must be revised
   825	to match." Both are done.)*
   826	D-2 Unicode 16.0.0 is pinned. Unassigned code points are rejected, and text that is not NFC is rejected rather than
   827	normalized.
   828	D-3 Limits: a payload of at most 2^24 bytes; depth 64 for the payload and 62 for content.
   829	D-4 Widths: a u8 tag length, a u16 canonicalization version equal to 1, and a u32 length (G-01 changed from u64).
   830	D-5 One tag for both signers' frames (A5 §A5.4). Slots are told apart by the fingerprint and the manifest order,
   831	and no slot name is written.
   832	D-6 Fingerprint strings carry scheme prefixes: `spki-sha256` for kms and `gpg-sha256` for gpg.
   833	**Owner decision (2026-10-09): no SHA-1 in V1.** The gpg key is bound by SHA-256 over its OpenPGP v4 public-key
   834	packet body, not by the v4 SHA-1 fingerprint, and this applies to the frame, the manifest and the DNS record. The
   835	KMS key stays SHA-256 of its SPKI. Its OpenPGP creation time is unbound, and that is accepted because the
   836	verifier binds the SPKI for that slot (§3.3).
   837	D-7 `manifest_sha256` is computed over the manifest frame. Spec, vectors and preamble are bound by SHA-256 of their
   838	raw bytes.
   839	D-8 *(revised 2026-10-12, findings 4 and 10)* The anchor preimage `A_sig` holds each slot's full frame and its
   840	exact detached signature, in manifest slot order, with a count byte, under the new tag
   841	`tessera-v1/receipt-anchor-sig`. The 2026-10-09 frame-only layout and its tag are withdrawn, with no fallback
   842	(§5.1). The DNSSEC snapshot has its own tagged envelope.
   843	D-9 A closed schema. `declared_issue_time` has second precision with no leap second. D is at most 241 characters.
   844	`binding_forms` is a sorted array.
   845	D-10 OpenPGP signature type 0x00 only. Since 2026-10-12, the anchor codec enforces this (§5.2, rule O).
   846	D-11 The dispatch boundary in §8.
   847	D-12 The DNS record: one RR, one string, a fixed order, no spaces, and the owner name compared without regard to
   848	case. Since 2026-10-12 every field is mandatory, so every canonical value is 255 bytes.
   849	D-13 Reason codes, and the order in which they are reported (§1.4, §3.1, §5, §7). "Trailing bytes" and
   850	"length-prefix mismatch" are reported as `TRAILING` or `TRUNCATED`, and this now includes a signature's own
   851	extent against its slot length. New in 2026-10-12:
   852	- `SIG_EMPTY`, `SIG_PACKET` and `SIG_FORMAT` (§5.2);
   853	- `LENGTH_CAP` for a signature above its cap;
   854	- the verification-stage codes of §5.3;
   855	- `DNS_WINDOW` (§7) and the C4 code `ISSUANCE_WINDOW` (§7.1);
   856	- `DNS_WINDOW_LENGTH` (§7; Amendment 9 disposition, 2026-10-12).
   857	
   858	D-14 *(2026-10-12)* Signature-slot rules are chosen by `(common.alg, slot)`. Rule O is one legacy-header binary v4
   859	packet of at most 1024 bytes. Rule R is raw FIPS 204/205 bytes of the exact parameter-set length. An unregistered
   860	id gets opaque bytes, 1 to 65536 of them.
   861	D-15 *(2026-10-12, finding 6)* The issue order is: frames, sign, verify, freeze the signature bytes, then stamp
   862	`A_sig` (§5.4).
   863	D-16 *(2026-10-12, finding 9)* The git channel's OTS stamp targets the signed commit's object id, never a tree, a
   864	blob or an unsigned commit (§7.2).
   865	D-17 *(2026-10-12, follow-up owner decision)* The issuance window lives in the key-publication record, in both
   866	channels. A receipt is `INVALID / ISSUANCE_WINDOW` unless not-before ≤ declared time and T ≤ not-after. T is the
   867	registered header-time-derived T, with no margin. Key-format expiries are not relied on (§7.1). *Revised
   868	2026-10-12 (Amendment 9 disposition):* both bounds are mandatory and the window is at most 90 days
   869	(`DNS_WINDOW_LENGTH`).
   870	
   871	**Out of P8's scope, and named so that nothing is dropped quietly:**
   872	- the bundle's container format and transport encoding (G-26);
   873	- where the observed anchor delay is recorded (G-40);
   874	- the snapshot body's format;
   875	- the git blob path;
   876	- the signature verification profile (P3 [assumption]). P8 now fixes each signature's *serialization* and its
   877	  place in `A_sig` (§5.2). Whether a signature verifies, and what that proves, remains P3's question.
   878	- the PQ algorithm id(s), the parameter set and the signing mode. They are registered only if the 21 Oct spike
   879	  passes (§5.2, rule R); *[OWNER 2026-10-19 annotation: superseded for the hybrid branch by HYBRID-SUPPLEMENT §1–§2,
   880	  registered 2026-10-16 (pair id, ML-DSA-65, pure, empty context). The bullet is kept as baseline text, as the
   881	  supplement directs.]*
   882	- which binding forms V1 requires.

